# For your current ecommerce project

I would **not create 10 packages immediately**.

Start with:

```text
ecommerce-nodejs/
│
├── apps/
│   ├── user-service/
│   ├── product-service/
│   └── order-service/
│
├── packages/
│   ├── logger/
│   ├── config/
│   ├── http-client/
│   └── validation/
│
├── helm/
│   └── ecommerce1.1/
│
├── package.json
├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
└── pnpm-lock.yaml
```

Then as the application grows:

```text
packages/
├── logger/          # Logging
├── config/          # Environment/configuration
├── http-client/     # Common HTTP calls
├── validation/      # Request validation
├── types/           # Shared TypeScript types
├── auth/            # Authentication/JWT
├── database/        # Common DB infrastructure
└── api-contracts/   # API request/response contracts
```

### A good architecture principle

Think of `packages/` as your **internal company-wide npm registry**:

```text
                    pnpm workspace
                         │
              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
              │                     │
             apps                packages
              │                     │
       ┌──────┼──────┐       ┌──────┼──────┐
       │      │      │       │      │      │
      user  product order   logger config validation
```

The major advantage is that when you fix something in `packages/logger`, **all services can consume the same shared implementation** without copying code between repositories.

-------------------------------------
app/service
  "dependencies": {
    "@packages/logger": "workspace:*",
    "@packages/config": "workspace:*"
  }

It means:
  user-service depends on two local packages inside the same pnpm workspace: logger and config.


And logger/package.json says:
{
  "name": "@packages/logger",
  "version": "1.0.0"
}

Then this:
  "@packages/logger": "workspace:*"

means:
    Find the workspace package whose name is @packages/logger.

    user-service
        |
        | workspace:*
        ↓
    packages/logger

  pnpm creates the appropriate workspace link between them.

  So when you write:

    import { logger } from "@packages/logger";

    Node/pnpm resolves it to your local:

    packages/logger/src/index.js
